    "listened": false,
    "transcript": "Two cautions: on the negative numbers themselves, the usual convention assigns the angle pi, so the principal value exists there too. But it jumps. Just below the cut, the angles are close to minus pi. And the principal logarithm undoes the exponential from one side only. Exponentiating the logarithm of z gives back z for every non-zero z. But the logarithm of e to the w gives back w only when the height of w is above minus pi and at most pi. That is the strip, with its top edge added by the convention.",
